ac{1^2}{3} + \\frac{2^2}{5} + \\frac{3^2}{7} +  \\; \\dots \\; + \\frac{1001^2}{2003}.\\]\n</p><p>Find the integer closest to $a-b.$\n</p><p>$\\text{(A) }500 \\qquad \\text{(B) }501 \\qquad \\text{(C) }999 \\qquad \\text{(D) }1000 \\qquad \\text{(E) }1001$\n</p>","html_includes_choices":true,"source":{"dataset":"amc10_atlas","official_live_verified":false,"record_id":"2002P-23","url":"https://github.com/liyong03/amc10-atlas"},"text_latex":"Let \\[a=\\frac{1^2}{1} + \\frac{2^2}{3} + \\frac{3^2}{5} + \\; \\dots \\; + \\frac{1001^2}{2001}\\] and \\[b=\\frac{1^2}{3} + \\frac{2^2}{5} + \\frac{3^2}{7} + \\; \\dots \\; + \\frac{1001^2}{2003}.\\] Find the integer closest to $a-b.$"},"year":2002},{"answer":{"accepted":["D"],"display":"D","source":{"dataset":"amc10_atlas","official_live_verified":false,"record_id":"2002P-24","url":"https://github.com/liyong03/amc10-atlas"},"type":"choice","verification":"single_source_or_disputed"},"assets":[],"choices":[{"html":"<div class=\"latex-text\">$14$</div>","label":"A","text_latex":"$14$"},{"html":"<div class=\"latex-text\">$15$</div>","label":"B","text_latex":"$15$"},{"html":"<div class=\"latex-text\">$16$</div>","label":"C","text_latex":"$16$"},{"html":"<div class=\"latex-text\">$17$</div>","label":"D","text_latex":"$17$"},{"html":"<div class=\"latex-text\">$18$</div>","label":"E","text_latex":"$18$"}],"contest":"AMC10","exam_id":"amc10-2002-regular-p","id":"amc10-2002-regular-p-24","number":24,"paper":"P","provenance":[{"dataset":"amc10_atlas","official_live_verified":false,"record_id":"2002P-24","url":"https://github.com/liyong03/amc10-atlas"}],"quality":{"auto_gradable":true,"flags":["missing_solution"],"ready_for_practice":true,"solutions_reviewed":false,"structurally_complete":true},"question_type":"multiple_choice","season":"regular","solutions":[],"source_url":"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php/2002_AMC_10P_Problems/Problem_24","special_paper":true,"statement":{"html":"<p>What is the maximum value of $n$ for which there is a set of distinct positive integers $k_1, k_2, ... k_n$ for which\n</p><p>\\[k^2_1 + k^2_2 + ... + k^2_n = 2002?\\]\n</p><p>$\\text{(A) }14 \\qquad \\text{(B) }15 \\qquad \\text{(C) }16 \\qquad \\text{(D) }17 \\qquad \\text{(E) }18$\n</p>","html_includes_choices":true,"source":{"dataset":"amc10_atlas","official_live_verified":false,"record_id":"2002P-24","url":"https://github.com/liyong03/amc10-atlas"},"text_latex":"What is the maximum value of $n$ for which there is a set of distinct positive integers $k_1, k_2, ... k_n$ for which \\[k^2_1 + k^2_2 + ... + k^2_n = 2002?\\]"},"year":2002},{"answer":{"accepted":["D"],"display":"D","source":{"dataset":"amc10_atlas","official_live_verified":false,"record_id":"2002P-25","url":"https://github.com/liyong03/amc10-atlas"},"type":"choice","verification":"single_source_or_disputed"},"assets":[],"choices":[{"html":"<div class=\"latex-text\">$175$</div>","label":"A","text_latex":"$175$"},{"html":"<div class=\"latex-text\">$179.5$</div>","label":"B","text_latex":"$179.5$"},{"html":"<div class=\"latex-text\">$182$</div>","label":"C","text_latex":"$182$"},{"html":"<div class=\"latex-text\">$188.5$</div>","label":"D","text_latex":"$188.5$"},{"html":"<div class=\"latex-text\">$201$</div>","label":"E","text_latex":"$201$"}],"contest":"AMC10","exam_id":"amc10-2002-regular-p","id":"amc10-2002-regular-p-25","number":25,"paper":"P","provenance":[{"dataset":"amc10_atlas","official_live_verified":false,"record_id":"2002P-25","url":"https://github.com/liyong03/amc10-atlas"}],"quality":{"auto_gradable":true,"flags":["missing_solution"],"ready_for_practice":true,"solutions_reviewed":false,"structurally_complete":true},"question_type":"multiple_choice","season":"regular","solutions":[],"source_url":"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php/2002_AMC_10P_Problems/Problem_25","special_paper":true,"statement":{"html":"<p>Under the new AMC $10, 12$ scoring method, $6$ points are given for each correct answer, $2.5$ points are given for each unanswered question, and no points are given for an incorrect answer. Some of the possible scores between $0$ and $150$ can be obtained in only one way, for example, the only way to obtain a score of $146.5$ is to have $24$ correct answers and one unanswered question. Some scores can be obtained in exactly two ways, for example, a score of $104.5$ can be obtained with $17$ correct answers, $1$ unanswered question, and $7$ incorrect, and also with $12$ correct answers and $13$ unanswered questions. There are (three) scores that can be obtained in exactly three ways. What is their sum?\n</p><p><br>\n$\\text{(A) }175 \\qquad \\text{(B) }179.5 \\qquad \\text{(C) }182 \\qquad \\text{(D) }188.5 \\qquad \\text{(E) }201$\n</p>","html_includes_choices":true,"source":{"dataset":"amc10_atlas","official_live_verified":false,"record_id":"2002P-25","url":"https://github.com/liyong03/amc10-atlas"},"text_latex":"Under the new AMC $10, 12$ scoring method, $6$ points are given for each correct answer, $2.5$ points are given for each unanswered question, and no points are given for an incorrect answer. Some of the possible scores between $0$ and $150$ can be obtained in only one way, for example, the only way to obtain a score of $146.5$ is to have $24$ correct answers and one unanswered question. Some scores can be obtained in exactly two ways, for example, a score of $104.5$ can be obtained with $17$ correct answers, $1$ unanswered question, and $7$ incorrect, and also with $12$ correct answers and $13$ unanswered questions. There are (three) scores that can be obtained in exactly three ways. What is their sum?"},"year":2002}],"revision":"3401fb112f97aa2b741faa8d15181f3b4342c9eadc3fe20f45559919f4245ac0"}
